PARITY reproduced from the web source (THE spec)
===========================================================================
  - A sort-field dropdown (web `<Select size="sm">`) next to a direction toggle (web `<button>` with an
    up/down arrow), composed inline (web `inline-flex items-center gap-1`); controlled component — caller
    owns `field` + `direction`, every change reported via `onFieldChange` / `onDirectionChange` (the
    surface stores nothing).
  - Field dropdown → SwiftUI `Menu` (the HIG-idiomatic pop-up selector; sibling idiom PlaybackSpeedMenu):
    trigger shows the selected option's label + an up/down chevron; one Button per option with a checkmark
    on the selected field (web `<option selected>`). aria-label = `t('sortControl.fieldLabel','Sort by')`,
    value = selected label.
  - Direction toggle → a square SF Symbol Button: `arrow.up` for 'asc' / `arrow.down` for 'desc' (web
    ArrowUp/ArrowDown); a tap flips the direction (web `flip = direction === 'asc' ? 'desc' : 'asc'`),
    reported already-flipped through `onDirectionChange`. Ported verbatim in SortControlProjector.toggled,
    unit-tested both ways.
  - i18n: sortControl.ascending "Ascending" / sortControl.descending "Descending" (the dirLabel + button
    title), sortControl.fieldLabel "Sort by" (dropdown a11y name), sortControl.direction "Sort direction"
    (composed into the button a11y name `${direction}: ${dirLabel}`). The web
    `directionAriaLabel ?? `${t('sortControl.direction')}: ${dirLabel}`` override is reproduced. All via the
    P1/S10 facade; no English literals in code.
  - testId → accessibility identifiers (base = testId|slug, field = `${base}-field`, direction =
    `${base}-direction`, web `${testId}-field` / `${testId}-direction`).
  - Generic note: web is generic over `F extends string`; the native peer uses a plain `String` field key
    (the stable value carried in URL state), preserving the value/label option list + selection semantics.

  REAL states reproduced (faithful-parity note): the web <SortControl> is a PURE, CONTROLLED presentational
  component — only hook is useTranslation, no fetch / React-Query / Promise — so it has NO loading / error /
  stale / offline branch. Inventing such chrome would FABRICATE states the source does not have (same
  reasoning as the accepted sibling presentational primitives DensityToggle-0153, ActiveFilterChips-0147).
  Reproduced branches: populated field dropdown with one option selected, the field-not-in-options edge
  (trigger falls back to the raw field key, never blank), the ascending / descending toggle, the
  custom-vs-default direction accessibility label, and the degenerate empty-options case (the dropdown
  becomes a friendly labelled empty chip — HIG: never a bare box — while the independent direction toggle
  keeps rendering, exactly as the web renders the button unconditionally), plus VoiceOver / Dynamic Type /
  Reduce-Motion.

